From 2f4b8d514ee6316a09d65e579562bb53d6234f55 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Lakr <git@qaq.wiki>
Date: Mon, 9 Nov 2026 05:04:54 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 07/24] Quarantine both type-11 mappings that claim one guest
 page

On macOS 27 the guest handed a live display surface's pages (mid=2) to
the kernel and to new surfaces without any UNMAP for it. The device saw
the new surfaces collide with mid=2, closed the new mappings, and went on
flushing frames through mid=2 into what was by then the kernel's memory.
The old surface's table re-read identically, so nothing on the device side
said it was stale; nothing said the new one was either.

A collision now quarantines both mappings' current incarnations. A page
proof covers neither, including one minted before the collision, so every
CPU flush, import write and direct write refuses for the whole mapping;
GPU writes already submitted to either page list are waited for; their
owed frames stay in the ledger unwritten. Re-reading the same table lifts
nothing. A quarantine lifts only on protocol evidence about that mapping:
its UNMAP, a new MAP or MappingInternal for its id, or its backing's
delete, which also drops its frame unwritten; a partner left without a
counterpart lifts with it and keeps its frame. surface_quarantine logs
both sides' ids, generations, time since their last adopted table, owed
frames and in-flight writes, and the shared page; surface_quarantine_lifted
logs the evidence.

The mapper ring now also counts, always on, a backlog, an overrun and a
consumer ahead of the producer, and logs a ring read that fails, after
which the rest of the backlog, an UNMAP included, waits for the next
publish.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
(cherry picked from commit 8beb95da6126e88b60135517e74f720095dbf0b0)
